1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,068
The worker died after saving an approval.

2
00:00:03,168 --> 00:00:06,000
The business outcome still happened only once.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:11,540
A supplier approval cannot disappear, or be applied twice.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:16,008
This project keeps that decision intact when execution fails.

5
00:00:16,833 --> 00:00:21,223
Start with one supplier. Policy evidence informs the review.

6
00:00:21,323 --> 00:00:25,136
An authenticated person decides whether to approve.

7
00:00:25,967 --> 00:00:29,396
The operator approves the exact request version.

8
00:00:29,496 --> 00:00:34,056
That authority stays separate from model advice and workflow retries.

9
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:38,716
The records are saved, but completion is not acknowledged.

10
00:00:38,816 --> 00:00:42,267
A crash here must never repeat the business action.

11
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:47,460
A replacement worker resumes the same workflow.

12
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,885
Temporal retries the activity, without another human approval.

13
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:57,580
The history confirms a second activity attempt and a completed workflow.

14
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:01,131
Recovery is visible in the orchestration record.

15
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:08,136
The database still holds one approval, one supplier record, and one local
notification.

16
00:01:08,236 --> 00:01:11,665
Two attempts produced one committed business outcome.

17
00:01:12,467 --> 00:01:18,349
Temporal handles durable coordination. Postgres enforces business identity.

18
00:01:18,449 --> 00:01:22,433
Model advice has no authority to approve the supplier.

19
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:28,412
Clone the repository to create a supplier and reproduce the crash locally.

20
00:01:28,512 --> 00:01:32,709
The default demonstration needs no paid model credentials.
